---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import DnaSim from '../../simulators/dna/DnaSim.astro';
import { dnaQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/dna';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What DNA replication is' },
  { id: 'structure', label: 'DNA structure in brief' },
  { id: 'steps', label: 'Step by step' },
  { id: 'leading-lagging', label: 'Leading and lagging strands' },
  { id: 'semi', label: 'Semi-conservative replication' },
  { id: 'accuracy', label: 'Speed and accuracy' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 14.4: DNA Replication in Prokaryotes. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-4-dna-replication-in-prokaryotes' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 14.3: Basics of DNA Replication. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-3-basics-of-dna-replication' },
  { text: 'Meselson, M., & Stahl, F. W. (1958). The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli. PNAS, 44(7), 671–682.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.44.7.671' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="dna-replication"
  description="Interactive DNA replication simulator: watch helicase unzip the helix, see the leading strand built continuously and the lagging strand in Okazaki fragments with primers and ligase."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={dnaQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every time a cell divides, it must copy all of its DNA, accurately, in a matter of hours. Watch a replication fork
      in action: helicase unzips the helix, primase lays primers, DNA polymerase builds the new strands, and ligase stitches
      the fragments together.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <DnaSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong> and watch the fork move to the right as <strong>helicase</strong> unzips the helix.</li>
      <li>Follow the <strong>leading strand</strong> (bottom): it is built continuously, right behind the fork.</li>
      <li>Follow the <strong>lagging strand</strong> (top): primase lays a pink RNA primer, DNA polymerase builds a fragment backwards, then the primer is swapped for DNA and <strong>ligase</strong> seals the gap.</li>
      <li><strong>Click any base</strong> to see its name, its partner and which strand it belongs to. <strong>Click an enzyme</strong> to learn its job.</li>
      <li>Slow it down with <strong>Speed</strong> to watch each step. <strong>Reset</strong> starts again with a new random sequence.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What DNA replication is</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>DNA replication</strong> is the process of copying a DNA molecule before a cell divides, so each new cell
    receives a complete set of genetic instructions. It happens during the S phase of the cell cycle. In human cells, about
    6 billion base pairs are copied, starting at thousands of points called <strong>origins of replication</strong> so the
    job finishes in hours rather than weeks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="structure">DNA structure in brief</h2>
  <p>
    DNA is a double helix: two strands, each a chain of nucleotides with a sugar-phosphate <strong>backbone</strong> and a
    <strong>base</strong> (A, T, G or C). The bases pair across the middle by hydrogen bonds: <strong>A with T</strong> and
    <strong>G with C</strong>. The strands are <strong>antiparallel</strong>: one runs 5′→3′ and its partner runs 3′→5′,
    which is why the labels at the ends of the strands in the simulator alternate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="steps">Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Unwinding:</strong> helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between bases, opening a Y-shaped <strong>replication fork</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Priming:</strong> primase makes a short <strong>RNA primer</strong> (3 bases in the model; about 10 in real cells). DNA polymerase cannot start a strand from nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Elongation:</strong> DNA polymerase III adds nucleotides that pair with the template, always to the 3′ end of the growing strand.</li>
    <li><strong>Primer removal:</strong> DNA polymerase I removes the RNA primers and fills the gaps with DNA.</li>
    <li><strong>Joining:</strong> DNA ligase seals the remaining nicks in the backbone, producing continuous strands.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Other proteins help too: single-strand binding proteins stop the separated strands from re-pairing, and topoisomerase
    relieves the twisting that builds up ahead of the fork. They are left out of the model to keep it clear.
  </p>

  <h2 id="leading-lagging">Leading and lagging strands</h2>
  <p>
    DNA polymerase can only build in the <strong>5′→3′ direction</strong>. Because the two templates are antiparallel, on one
    of them that direction follows the fork: the <strong>leading strand</strong> is made continuously from a single primer.
    On the other, 5′→3′ points away from the fork, so the <strong>lagging strand</strong> is built in short pieces called
    <strong>Okazaki fragments</strong>, each with its own primer. In bacteria they are 1,000–2,000 bases long; in
    eukaryotes, about 100–200.
  </p>

  <h2 id="semi">Semi-conservative replication</h2>
  <p>
    When copying finishes, each new molecule has one <strong>original strand</strong> (blue in the simulator) and one
    <strong>new strand</strong> (orange). This is called <strong>semi-conservative</strong> replication. Matthew Meselson and
    Franklin Stahl proved it in 1958 by growing bacteria in heavy nitrogen, moving them to normal nitrogen, and measuring the
    density of the DNA after each generation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Speed and accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    Bacterial replication forks move at around 1,000 bases per second; human forks at about 50. DNA polymerase also
    <strong>proofreads</strong>: it can remove a wrongly paired base immediately. Together with later repair systems, the
    final error rate is roughly one mistake per billion bases copied. The rare errors that slip through are
    <strong>mutations</strong>, which are the raw material for natural selection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Template strand</dt>
    <dd>An existing strand used as the pattern for building a new one.</dd>
    <dt>Replication fork</dt>
    <dd>The Y-shaped region where the helix is being unzipped and copied.</dd>
    <dt>Okazaki fragment</dt>
    <dd>A short stretch of new DNA on the lagging strand.</dd>
    <dt>Primer</dt>
    <dd>A short RNA strand that gives DNA polymerase a 3′ end to build onto.</dd>
    <dt>5′ and 3′ ends</dt>
    <dd>The two chemically different ends of a strand, named after carbon atoms in the sugar.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The new DNA molecule is completely new.”</strong> Each daughter molecule keeps one original strand.</li>
    <li><strong>“The lagging strand is made more slowly or later.”</strong> Both strands are made at the same overall rate; the lagging one is just made in pieces.</li>
    <li><strong>“DNA polymerase can start a strand anywhere.”</strong> It needs a primer with a free 3′ end.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Helicase unzips; primase primes; DNA polymerase builds; ligase joins.</li>
    <li>Bases pair A–T and G–C, so each strand is a template for its partner.</li>
    <li>Polymerase only works 5′→3′, giving a continuous leading strand and a fragmented lagging strand.</li>
    <li>Replication is semi-conservative: each copy is half old, half new.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: one fork copying 40 base pairs, greatly slowed down. The two primers left at the ends are removed by
    enzymes from the neighbouring fork in a real chromosome, and at chromosome tips by telomerase.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
